These commands are recipes, not evidence that this component passed.

- static: npm run type:check
- browser: npm run dev; open /design-system#activity-log and exercise the documented interaction.

# Alert Dialog

Confirm an irreversible consequence before performing it.

## Choose this component

Choose AlertDialog when a specific irreversible consequence needs confirmation. Use Dialog for editable tasks and an inline action for easily reversible work.

## Usage rules

### alert-dialog-usage

Owner: alert-dialog.

When deleting a named resource requires an asynchronous request.

Do: Name the resource and consequence, offer Cancel before submission, and close only after the request succeeds. Keep cancellation available while pending only if the host can actually cancel the operation.

Avoid: Use a generic Are you sure prompt whose action closes before the request settles.

Why: The user needs the exact consequence and a retry path when the deletion fails.

Checked by: review only. Look for: Confirm a failed asynchronous revocation; the dialog and error remain. Cancel works before submission, and pending cancellation is offered only if it actually stops the operation.

Source evidence:
- src/components/v4/ui/alert-dialog.tsx
- src/pages/design-system/examples/alert-dialog/default.tsx

## State guidance

- pending: Control open state and prevent repeated destructive submission.
- error: Keep the dialog open with the failed action and recovery text.

## Presentation rules

Do: Label the action Delete connection or Revoke access.

Avoid: Label both trigger and confirmation Continue.

Why: Vague labels hide which irreversible operation is being approved.

## Import and exports

- src/components/v4/ui/alert-dialog.tsx
- src/components/v4/hooks/use-discard-changes-guard.ts
- src/components/v4/lib/overlay-pending.ts

Exports: AlertDialog, AlertDialogAction, AlertDialogCancel, AlertDialogContent, AlertDialogDescription, AlertDialogFooter, AlertDialogHeader, AlertDialogMedia, AlertDialogOverlay, AlertDialogPortal, AlertDialogTitle, AlertDialogTrigger, DiscardChangesAlertDialog, useDiscardChangesGuard. Source TypeScript defines the API.

## Behavior

- Controlled open/onOpenChange or defaultOpen owns visibility. Action and Cancel are Radix close controls; an async operation must explicitly control closing while it runs.
- AlertDialog renders at most one lead tile. tone danger shows a 40px bad-bg tile with the trash icon; icon swaps the glyph and AlertDialogMedia replaces the tile entirely, tinted by tone. With tone neutral or no tone there is no automatic tile; an icon or AlertDialogMedia there gets the neutral tint tile. The destructive action is the solid danger Button: bad-fill with white on-bad text in both themes, the neutral disabled control when disabled.
- confirmText adds a mono field, labeled Type <name> to confirm and focused on open, above the footer; the action stays disabled until the field matches exactly. Enter in the field does nothing until it matches; once it matches, Enter clicks the first AlertDialogAction, the same as a click, so a loading action takes over from there and a pending dialog ignores Enter.
- One pad (space-16) on all four sides. Header, body and footer stack with the content gap and no margins. The footer has no band or rule: the actions sit right-aligned on the panel. A failure shows as a bad Alert in the body above the footer.
- When AlertDialogHeader or AlertDialogFooter is a direct child, the lead tile, header and body share one scroll region with the edge fade, and the footer stays pinned below it; the footer's own pad is the space above the actions.
- pending on AlertDialogContent blocks Escape and disables AlertDialogCancel while an action runs; the content is aria-busy.
- loading on AlertDialogAction follows Button's loading: the spinner replaces the label at the same width, the action stays focusable and aria-busy, and further clicks and Enter are ignored, so a confirmation cannot submit twice. With asChild the composed child gets the same guard, aria-busy and aria-disabled, so its own onClick cannot fire while loading; give the child loading too for the spinner. While loading is true the content is pending as above. Passing loading at all, even false, hands closing to the host: a click runs onClick and leaves the dialog open, and the host sets open false once the request succeeds.
- DiscardChangesAlertDialog is the v3 discard prompt: a danger alert with the alert icon, title (default Discard changes?), description (default Your changes will be lost.), Keep editing and a destructive Discard that calls onDiscard. useDiscardChangesGuard({ isDirty, onDiscard }) returns the v3 shape (requestDiscard, confirmDiscard, isDiscardDialogOpen, setIsDiscardDialogOpen) plus discardDialogProps to spread on the prompt. requestDiscard calls onDiscard at once when clean and opens the prompt when dirty; route a Dialog or Sheet onOpenChange(false) through it.

## Accessibility

- Title and Description name the alert dialog. Focus starts on Cancel and returns to the trigger on close. The dialog stays inside the viewport, and Escape inside an open editor closes that editor first.
- A focus ring is never clipped where the scroll region meets the footer: the region keeps the ring's reach (size-focus-ring outline past an equal offset, or size-focus-glow, whichever is larger) clear below its last control, such as the confirmText field, without moving the footer, and Tab scrolls a control that far inside either edge.

## Use cases

- For an async action, pass loading to AlertDialogAction and control open state, retain the resource and consequence text, and close only after the host confirms success.

## Avoid

- Assume AlertDialogAction waits for a promise or close the confirmation before a failure can be shown.

## Limits

- Action does not await a request or infer success; pass loading (or prevent its default close) and control open while an async operation settles.

## Declared example coverage

Source declarations and review criteria only. Generation checks references and completeness, not rendered behavior or API parity.

### AlertDialogContent.size

- default: example `sizes`, visible label "default".
- sm: example `sizes`, visible label "sm".

### AlertDialogContent.tone

- danger: example `confirm`, visible label "danger".
- neutral: example `confirm`, visible label "neutral".

## Executable examples

### Confirmation states

Save this example at `src/pages/design-system/examples/alert-dialog/default.tsx`; its relative imports are generated for that exact path. States: default, loading, error, keyboard.

Registry items used: @cipher/alert, @cipher/alert-dialog, @cipher/button. Additional packages: none. Install every listed item before compiling this example.

```tsx
import { useState } from "react";

import { Alert, AlertDescription } from "../../../../components/v4/ui/alert";
import * as UI from "../../../../components/v4/ui/alert-dialog";
import { Button } from "../../../../components/v4/ui/button";

export default function Example() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState<string | null>(null);
  return (
    <div className="grid w-full min-w-0 grid-cols-[repeat(auto-fit,minmax(min(100%,8rem),1fr))] items-start justify-items-center gap-x-6 gap-y-8">
      {(["Confirmation", "Pending", "Error"] as const).map((state) => (
        <figure
          key={state}
          className="m-0 flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col items-center gap-3 text-center"
        >
          <div className="flex min-h-8 w-full min-w-0 flex-col items-center gap-3">
            <UI.AlertDialog
              open={open === state}
              onOpenChange={(next) => setOpen(next ? state : null)}
            >
              <UI.AlertDialogTrigger asChild>
                <Button variant="outline">Revoke access</Button>
              </UI.AlertDialogTrigger>
              <UI.AlertDialogContent tone="danger">
                <UI.AlertDialogHeader>
                  <UI.AlertDialogTitle>Revoke client access?</UI.AlertDialogTitle>
                  <UI.AlertDialogDescription>
                    This client will no longer read Staging secrets.
                  </UI.AlertDialogDescription>
                </UI.AlertDialogHeader>
                {state === "Error" && (
                  <Alert tone="bad">
                    <AlertDescription>Access could not be revoked. Try again.</AlertDescription>
                  </Alert>
                )}
                <UI.AlertDialogFooter>
                  <UI.AlertDialogCancel>Cancel</UI.AlertDialogCancel>
                  <UI.AlertDialogAction asChild>
                    <Button variant="destructive" loading={state === "Pending"}>
                      Revoke access
                    </Button>
                  </UI.AlertDialogAction>
                </UI.AlertDialogFooter>
              </UI.AlertDialogContent>
            </UI.AlertDialog>
          </div>
          <figcaption className="text-muted-foreground text-xs">{state}</figcaption>
        </figure>
      ))}
    </div>
  );
}
```

### Action loading

Save this example at `src/pages/design-system/examples/alert-dialog/loading.tsx`; its relative imports are generated for that exact path. States: default, loading.

Registry items used: @cipher/alert-dialog, @cipher/button. Additional packages: none. Install every listed item before compiling this example.

```tsx
import { useEffect, useRef, useState } from "react";

import * as UI from "../../../../components/v4/ui/alert-dialog";
import { Button } from "../../../../components/v4/ui/button";

export default function Example() {
  const [open, setOpen] = useState(false);
  const [deleting, setDeleting] = useState(false);
  const [deleted, setDeleted] = useState(false);
  const timer = useRef<number | undefined>(undefined);
  useEffect(() => () => window.clearTimeout(timer.current), []);
  let label = "Ready";
  if (deleting) label = "Deleting";
  else if (deleted) label = "Deleted in this local example";
  return (
    <figure className="m-0 flex w-full min-w-0 flex-col gap-3">
      <div className="flex min-h-8 w-full min-w-0 flex-col items-start gap-3">
        <UI.AlertDialog open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen}>
          <UI.AlertDialogTrigger asChild>
            <Button variant="outline">Delete connection</Button>
          </UI.AlertDialogTrigger>
          <UI.AlertDialogContent tone="danger">
            <UI.AlertDialogHeader>
              <UI.AlertDialogTitle>Delete production-postgres?</UI.AlertDialogTitle>
              <UI.AlertDialogDescription>
                Syncs and rotations that use this connection stop.
              </UI.AlertDialogDescription>
            </UI.AlertDialogHeader>
            <UI.AlertDialogFooter>
              <UI.AlertDialogCancel>Cancel</UI.AlertDialogCancel>
              <UI.AlertDialogAction
                variant="destructive"
                loading={deleting}
                onClick={() => {
                  setDeleting(true);
                  timer.current = window.setTimeout(() => {
                    setDeleting(false);
                    setDeleted(true);
                    setOpen(false);
                  }, 2000);
                }}
              >
                Delete connection
              </UI.AlertDialogAction>
            </UI.AlertDialogFooter>
          </UI.AlertDialogContent>
        </UI.AlertDialog>
      </div>
      <figcaption className="text-muted-foreground text-xs">{label}</figcaption>
    </figure>
  );
}
```

